# Skill Usage Guide

## 1. What a Skill is

A Skill is a reusable work instruction for an AI role. It does not automatically load into every ChatGPT web chat merely because it exists in GitHub or on the user's computer.

For ChatGPT web:
1. open the Skill's `SKILL.md`;
2. attach or paste it into the new chat;
3. attach the task's Session Packet and required project documents;
4. explicitly ask the AI to follow that skill.

For OpenCode or another compatible agent, place the skill in its supported skills directory and verify discovery using that tool's current documentation.

## 2. Skill selection

| Work requested | Primary skill |
|---|---|
| Decide what product should do | product-strategist |
| Research users/competitors | product-researcher |
| Write testable requirements | requirements-engineer |
| Define entities and relationships | domain-modeler |
| Design screens and interactions | ux-architect |
| Design system/module boundaries | system-architect |
| Design SQL schema/RLS | database-architect |
| Define API/data interfaces | api-contract-designer |
| Implement web UI | frontend-engineer |
| Implement trusted server logic | backend-engineer |
| Verify behavior/security | qa-engineer |
| Update docs and registries | documentation-steward |
| Route tasks and enforce workflow | engineering-orchestrator |

## 3. Required input for every skill invocation

Never send only “use the Goal skill”. Supply:
- skill file;
- Master Blueprint;
- Current Status;
- parent module spec;
- assigned child spec or a request to create it;
- relevant contracts;
- relevant dependency entries;
- task brief and allowed scope.

## 4. Example: start a Goal module design chat

Attach:
- `engineering-orchestrator/SKILL.md`
- `domain-modeler/SKILL.md`
- `requirements-engineer/SKILL.md`
- `system-architect/SKILL.md`
- `MASTER_BLUEPRINT.md`
- `CURRENT_STATUS.md`
- `DECISION_LOG.md`
- `MOD-03_GOAL/MODULE.md` (if already created)
- relevant open questions and dependency registry

Prompt:
“Act as the FLOW-UP Engineering Orchestrator. This is a SPECIFICATION session for MOD-03 Goal Management. First inspect the supplied source files, identify missing/contradictory decisions, and propose a bounded decomposition. Do not write implementation code. Create child specification files using the provided templates. Do not change shared contracts without a Change Impact Review and explicit approval.”

## 5. Example: start an implementation chat

Attach:
- frontend-engineer or backend-engineer skill;
- Master Blueprint and Current Status;
- approved parent module specification;
- approved child specification;
- approved API/data contract;
- relevant design system and testing rules;
- implementation task packet.

Prompt:
“Act as FLOW-UP [Frontend/Backend] Engineer. Implement only task [TASK-ID] against specification [SPEC-ID, VERSION]. Do not reinterpret requirements or change shared contracts. First list the acceptance criteria and files you expect to touch. Wait for approval before implementation. Return full changed files, tests, and a completed handoff.”

## 6. Use the smallest useful skill set

Do not attach all 13 skills to every chat. Use one primary skill and only the supporting skills required for that task. Too many role instructions can conflict and waste context.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/00_CONTROL/SPECIFICATION_TREE_RULES.md">
# Specification Tree Rules

## 1. Where detail grows

A module's detail belongs inside its own folder, not in the Master Blueprint.

Example:
`docs/04_MODULES/MOD-03_GOAL/`

- `MODULE.md`: parent module overview, scope, child index, status.
- `requirements/`: use cases and functional requirements.
- `domain/`: entities, invariants, lifecycle, business rules.
- `architecture/`: component design and internal dependency.
- `database/`: tables, fields, constraints, indexes, RLS proposals.
- `api/`: endpoint/use-case contracts.
- `ui/`: screens, states, interactions, accessibility.
- `testing/`: test plan and cases.
- `decisions/`: module-local decisions.
- `implementation/`: implementation plan, file map, build notes.

## 2. Specification levels

- L0: product blueprint.
- L1: system area.
- L2: module.
- L3: submodule/component.
- L4: implementation unit.

Every child artifact must identify its parent ID. Every parent `MODULE.md` must index its children.

## 3. Stable IDs

Examples:
- `MOD-03`: Goal Management module.
- `MOD-03-DOM-001`: Goal domain model.
- `MOD-03-REQ-001`: Goal requirements.
- `MOD-03-API-001`: Goal API contract.
- `MOD-03-UI-001`: Goal List view.
- `MOD-03-TEST-001`: Goal test plan.
- `TASK-2026-001`: implementation task.

Do not reuse IDs. If an artifact is retired, mark it deprecated and retain its ID/history.

## 4. Child artifact metadata

Every specification begins with:
- ID
- Title
- Parent ID
- Version
- Status
- Owner
- Last updated
- Depends on
- Used by
- Related contracts
- Related requirements

## 5. Parent-child consistency

When a child spec is created or changed:
1. update the parent module's child index;
2. update dependency registry if cross-module dependencies change;
3. update contract registry if interfaces change;
4. update traceability matrix if requirements/tests change;
5. update current status;
6. create a change-impact record when shared behavior changes.

## 6. Do not over-decompose

Create a child specification when it has at least one of:
- independent responsibility;
- distinct contract;
- separate implementation owner/session;
- substantial business rules;
- meaningful independent test boundary.

Do not create a separate spec for every tiny function or CSS class. The objective is independent work without fragmenting the system into unmanageable pieces.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/00_CONTROL/UPDATE_AND_VERSIONING.md">
# Update and Versioning Procedure

## 1. Artifact status

`DRAFT → IN_REVIEW → APPROVED → IMPLEMENTED → VERIFIED → RELEASED`

Not every artifact uses every status. A design spec can be APPROVED before code exists. An implementation is VERIFIED only with test evidence.

## 2. Versioning
- Patch (0.1.0 → 0.1.1): clarification, typo, non-behavioral correction.
- Minor (0.1.0 → 0.2.0): additive requirement or compatible detail.
- Major (0.1.0 → 1.0.0): breaking contract, entity lifecycle, behavior, or compatibility change.

This is a project convention, not an external standard.

## 3. When a module detail changes
1. Copy/modify the relevant child spec.
2. Record what changed and why.
3. Check parent module and dependencies.
4. Run Change Impact Review.
5. Obtain approval for shared contract changes.
6. Update affected tests and traceability.
7. Update parent index and Current Status.
8. Commit with a meaningful message.

## 4. When implementation discovers a design defect
Do not silently patch code and leave docs unchanged.
- Open a design issue.
- Describe observed mismatch and evidence.
- Propose spec change.
- Identify affected modules/contracts.
- Obtain approval.
- Update spec first or in the same reviewed change.
- Implement and test.
- Record the final decision.

## 5. Git commit conventions
Examples:
- `docs: add MOD-03 goal domain specification`
- `docs: approve C-003 goal summary contract v1`
- `feat(goal): implement create and update use cases`
- `test(goal): add hierarchy invariant coverage`
- `fix(schedule): reject invalid time range`

## 6. Release snapshot
At each milestone, tag the repository and record:
- blueprint version;
- module spec versions;
- contract versions;
- database migration range;
- test results;
- known limitations.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/01_PRODUCT/MVP_SCOPE.md">
# MVP 0.1 Scope

**Product:** FLOW-UP  
**MVP Type:** Shared Web Demo  
**Status:** PROPOSED

## Goal

Demonstrate one complete, persistent, user-understandable goal execution loop using a shared workspace.

The MVP must help visitors turn an outcome into structured goals, milestones, scheduled actions, measurable progress, and review.

## Must Have

1. Provide one shared workspace accessible to all visitors.
2. No authentication, registration, or user management.
3. Create an outcome/goal directly inside the shared workspace.
4. Add child milestones to a goal in a hierarchy.
5. Browse the hierarchy in List/Outline view.
6. Optional Tree view for demonstration/testing.
7. Add a basic measurement to a goal: label, unit, current value, target value.
8. Record measurement updates and retain measurement history.
9. Create an action associated with a goal or milestone.
10. Give an action a status and optional start/end date and time.
11. Display scheduled actions in a simple calendar.
12. Mark an action complete.
13. Calculate and display transparent progress.
14. Show a basic review: target/plan vs actual.
15. Persist shared data across page refreshes, browser sessions, and different visitors.
16. Include seeded demo data and a reliable demo path.

## Should Have If Time Permits

- Simple dashboard with active goals and upcoming actions.
- Empty, loading, error, and success states.
- Basic responsive mobile web layout.
- Export or reset demo data.
- Clear indication that the workspace and its data are shared.

## Must Not Be Required for MVP

- Authentication, registration, or user management.
- Multiple workspaces or personal workspaces.
- Project management as a prerequisite for creating goals.
- Paid AI APIs or AI-generated plans.
- Team collaboration and role-based permissions.
- Custom table builder.
- User-authored arbitrary formulas.
- General algorithm marketplace.
- Advanced notifications.
- Native mobile app.
- Offline multi-device conflict resolution.
- Complex widget builder.

## MVP Acceptance Scenario

Given an accessible shared workspace:

1. A visitor opens the workspace without signing in.
2. The visitor creates a goal directly in the workspace.
3. The visitor creates at least one milestone under that goal.
4. The visitor creates an action associated with the goal or milestone.
5. The visitor schedules the action.
6. The visitor records a measurement update and/or completes the action.
7. The system persists the changes in the shared database.
8. The relevant progress view reflects the updated data.
9. Another browser or visitor can see the same updated data.
10. The visitor can understand what is next and whether the goal is on track.

## Demo Strategy

Prepare both:

- A live, short, end-to-end workflow using the shared workspace.
- A seeded workspace with realistic goal hierarchy, actions, schedule, measurement history, and progress.

The demo must not depend on live AI generation.

## Important Constraints

- This is a shared demonstration environment, not a private personal workspace.
- All visitors can potentially see and modify the same data.
- Do not store private or sensitive information.
- Public write access must be controlled through appropriate backend validation and database permissions.
- Technical implementation details remain subject to architecture and security decisions.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/01_PRODUCT/PRODUCT_HYPOTHESES.md">
# Product Hypotheses & Experiments

| ID | Hypothesis | Experiment | Evidence / metric | Decision rule |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| H-001 | Users understand outcome-first setup | 3-5 users create a goal | Completion rate, help requests, time | Revise if users repeatedly get stuck |
| H-002 | List/Outline is easier than Tree for editing | Same task in both views | Time, errors, preference | Keep Tree only if it adds measurable value |
| H-003 | Progress feedback helps users understand status | Ask users to interpret a progress panel | Correct interpretation, confidence | Simplify if interpretation is poor |
| H-004 | Scheduling actions closes planning-to-execution gap | User schedules a goal action | Successful scheduling, friction | Reduce steps if abandonment occurs |
| H-005 | Measurement history is useful | User updates a metric twice | Return behavior, perceived usefulness | Reassess if users do not understand metric |

Record participants with consent. Do not overgeneralize from a very small sample.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/01_PRODUCT/PRODUCT_VISION.md">
# Product Vision

## Vision
Help people close the gap between knowing what they want and consistently executing actions that produce measurable results.

## Initial target segment
Students, self-learners, freelancers, and individual knowledge workers managing personal goals or projects. This follows the submitted project description; the segment must still be validated through interviews and usage.

## User problem hypothesis
People can state goals but struggle to translate them into manageable actions, fit those actions into available time, and determine whether their approach is producing progress.

## Value proposition hypothesis
FLOW-UP connects:
- desired outcomes;
- milestones and hierarchy;
- measurable indicators;
- executable actions;
- schedules;
- actual progress and review.

## Positioning boundary
FLOW-UP is not attempting to reproduce every feature of ClickUp, Notion, or Asana. It focuses on a coherent personal goal-execution loop.

## Success evidence to collect
- Can a new user create a meaningful goal without assistance?
- Can they identify the next action?
- Can they schedule an action?
- Can they update actual progress?
- Can they understand whether they are on track?
- Do they return to update progress later?

Do not claim productivity improvement until measured in a suitable study.
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/02_DOMAIN/decisions/ADR-013_PROJECT_GOAL_RELATIONSHIP.md">
# ADR-013 — Project–Goal Relationship

**Status:** IN_REVIEW  
**Date opened:** 2026-11-16  
**Proposal owner:** Product owner

## Context

The initial product concept included Projects and Goals. However, requiring users to create a Project before creating a Goal introduces an additional step and may conflict with FLOW-UP's goal-first product direction.

The MVP now uses one shared Workspace without authentication. Goals should be created directly inside this Workspace.

The relationship between Project and Goal must be clarified before database and API contracts are finalized.

## Options Considered

### Option A — Project contains multiple Goals

- Workspace contains Projects.
- Each Project contains multiple Goals.
- Goals cannot exist outside a Project.

Advantages:
- Clear grouping for related goals.
- Project-level reporting can be introduced later.

Disadvantages:
- Adds a required concept before users can create a Goal.
- Increases MVP domain and UI complexity.

### Option B — Project is the root Goal

- Project and Goal share one conceptual hierarchy.
- A Project is treated as a special type of Goal.

Advantages:
- A single hierarchy model.
- Avoids separate Project-to-Goal relationships.

Disadvantages:
- Project semantics may differ from personal goals.
- Can complicate future reporting and project-specific behavior.

### Option C — Workspace contains Goals directly

- Workspace is the root container for MVP data.
- Goals are created directly inside Workspace.
- Project is not required in the MVP.
- Future Project grouping remains open.

Advantages:
- Supports goal-first product entry.
- Reduces setup steps and MVP complexity.
- Avoids introducing an unnecessary entity before its value is proven.

Disadvantages:
- Large numbers of Goals may require better filtering or grouping later.
- Project-level reporting is not available in MVP.
- Future Project support may require schema and contract changes.

## Proposed Decision

Option C.

- The MVP has one shared Workspace.
- Goals are created directly inside the Workspace.
- Project creation is not required before creating a Goal.
- Project is not a required parent of Goal in the MVP.
- Whether Project should exist in a later release remains open.

## Reasons

The MVP should validate the core goal execution loop before introducing additional organizational concepts.

The shared Workspace provides a single root container for all MVP data.

## Consequences / Trade-offs

- Workspace is the root data container.
- Goal hierarchy does not depend on a Project entity.
- Project-specific screens, relationships, and reporting are excluded from MVP.
- Future Project support may require domain and database changes.
- The domain model must not assume that Project is permanently removed from the product.

## Affected Modules / Contracts

- Workspace module
- Goal module
- Project module, if introduced in the future
- Database schema
- Goal creation and retrieval API
- Workspace data access rules

## Implementation Boundary

This ADR remains IN_REVIEW.

Do not treat this proposal as an approved implementation contract until the product owner explicitly approves it.

## Approval

**Status:** IN_REVIEW  
**Approver:** Pending  
**Date:** 2026-11-16
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/02_DOMAIN/decisions/ADR-014_SHARED_WORKSPACE_WITHOUT_AUTH.md">
# ADR-014 — Shared Workspace Without Authentication

**Status:** APPROVED  
**Date opened:** 2026-11-16  
**Decision date:** 2026-11-16  
**Approver:** Product owner

## Context

The initial architecture considered user accounts, authentication, and personal workspaces.

For the MVP, the product owner decided to prioritize product functionality and the core goal execution experience over account infrastructure.

The MVP is intended to be a shared web demonstration where visitors can access the same workspace and work with the same persistent dataset.

## Options Considered

### Option A — Personal Workspace with Authentication

- Each user registers and signs in.
- Each user owns a personal Workspace.
- Data is isolated by user.

Advantages:
- Supports private user data.
- Provides a foundation for multi-user product functionality.

Disadvantages:
- Requires authentication, user management, ownership relationships, and access control.
- Adds implementation and testing work before the core product is validated.

### Option B — One Shared Workspace Without Authentication

- Visitors access the application without signing in.
- All visitors use the same Workspace.
- All visitors work with the same dataset.

Advantages:
- Removes registration and login friction.
- Reduces MVP implementation scope.
- Allows the team to focus on the core product experience.
- Makes the demo straightforward.

Disadvantages:
- Data is not private.
- Visitors can affect each other's work.
- Public write access introduces abuse and data-integrity risks.
- Does not represent the eventual personal-workspace experience.

## Decision

Option B is approved for MVP.

- Provide exactly one shared Workspace.
- Do not implement authentication or registration.
- Do not implement user management.
- All visitors access the same shared dataset.
- Data is stored centrally and persists across page refreshes, browser sessions, and different visitors.
- Goals are created directly inside the shared Workspace.
- The MVP is a shared demonstration environment, not a private personal workspace.

## Reasons

The immediate priority is to validate whether FLOW-UP can help visitors move from outcomes to structured goals, actions, schedules, measurable progress, and review.

Authentication and personal data ownership are not necessary to demonstrate this core loop.

## Consequences / Trade-offs

### Product consequences

- No login or registration screens.
- No personal Workspace management.
- No user-specific data separation.
- All visitors can see the same data.
- All visitors can potentially modify the same data.
- The UI must clearly communicate that the Workspace is shared.

### Technical consequences

- MVP data does not require a User entity or user ownership relationship.
- A central database is required for persistent shared data.
- Backend validation is required for data integrity.
- Public write operations must be deliberately limited.
- Database credentials with elevated privileges must never be exposed in frontend code.
- Rate limiting, input validation, request-size limits, and abuse prevention must be considered.
- Seeded demo data and reset procedures must be designed carefully to avoid accidental data loss.

### Security and privacy constraints

- Do not store private or sensitive information.
- Do not assume that hiding a UI control provides access security.
- Do not expose database service-role or administrative credentials to visitors.
- Restrict database operations to the minimum permissions required by the application.
- The shared environment must be treated as publicly accessible.

## Affected Modules / Contracts

- Workspace module
- Goal module
- Action module
- Schedule module
- Measurement and Progress modules
- Database schema and access policies
- Backend API and validation
- Demo data and reset functionality
- Deployment configuration

## Implementation Boundary

This decision approves the product access model only.

It does not approve a specific backend framework, API architecture, database permission configuration, hosting provider, or security implementation.

Those technical decisions must be specified and reviewed separately.

## Future Reconsideration

Authentication and personal Workspaces may be introduced after the MVP validates the core product experience.

Such a change will require a separate decision and impact review.

## Approval

**Status:** APPROVED  
**Approver:** Product owner  
**Date:** 2026-11-16
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/02_DOMAIN/GLOSSARY.md">
# Domain Glossary

Terms are provisional until domain modeling is approved.

| Term | Definition | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Workspace | Personal container for projects and user-owned data | MVP may use one default workspace |
| Project | A bounded context for organizing a desired result | Exact distinction from Goal must be validated |
| Outcome / Goal | Desired end state that can be described and optionally measured | Avoid mixing with a task |
| Milestone | Meaningful intermediate result toward an outcome | May have parent goal |
| Action | Executable unit of work a user can perform | Can be scheduled |
| Measurement | Definition of a metric, unit, current value, target value | Metric type and aggregation require specification |
| Measurement Entry | Timestamped record of an observed value | Enables history |
| Schedule Entry | Time allocation for an action | Calendar is a view over schedule entries |
| Progress | Derived state based on explicit rules | Must be explainable and testable |
| Review | User-facing comparison of plan and actual | Does not autonomously alter plan |
| Hierarchy | Parent-child relationship among goals/milestones/actions | Not the same as cross-module dependency |
| Dependency | A relation indicating one item relies on another | Separate from parent-child |

Open domain questions:
- Is Project a container or itself a Goal?
- Can one Action contribute to multiple Goals?
- Can a Goal have multiple measurements?
- Can an Action have multiple schedule blocks?
- Are milestones and actions distinct entity types or one work-item type with a type field?
- What progress calculation applies to unmeasured goals?
</file>

<file path="FLOW-UP-Engineering-Blueprint-v0.1/docs/03_ARCHITECTURE/ARCHITECTURE_RULES.md">
# Architecture Rules

1. Use TypeScript strict mode for application code.
2. Domain rules must not live only inside React components.
3. UI modules call application interfaces; they do not own business data.
4. Database access is isolated behind repositories/adapters where this adds clarity.
5. Supabase is an infrastructure provider, not the domain model.
6. All user-owned data must have an explicit ownership path and RLS policy.
7. Never expose service-role keys to browser/mobile clients.
8. SQL migrations are the source of truth for schema changes.
9. Generated database types are generated artifacts, not manually maintained schema.
10. Tree/List/Calendar are projections over shared domain data.
11. Avoid circular dependencies.
12. Avoid microservices for MVP.
13. Do not add a dependency without documenting its purpose and license.
14. No paid AI API may be required for core product behavior.
15. Every calculation must have documented inputs, formula, rounding, edge cases, and tests.
16. Time values must define timezone handling; store instants consistently and render in the user's chosen/local timezone.
17. Destructive operations require explicit rules for children, linked actions, and schedule entries.
</file>

